00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.960
Speaker 1: Here's some interesting new polling&nbsp;
data since the draft from the Supreme Court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.600
leaked, which revealed to us that unless something&nbsp;
changes, Roe v Wade will be overturned in the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:20.000
few weeks. Since that leaked support for abortion&nbsp;
rights in most or all cases has gone up to 60%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.120
which is the highest level in two decades,&nbsp;
according to a new NBC News poll. Let's look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.640
some comments about the polling from Mark Murray,&nbsp;
who's the senior political editor for NBC News.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:38.880
Results from the poll. Abortion should be always&nbsp;
legal, say 37% of Americans legal most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:48.480
time say 23% of Americans. So in total, 60% of&nbsp;
Americans believe that abortion should be always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:55.760
or mostly legal. The combined 60% is the highest&nbsp;
percentage on this question, going all the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.080
back to 2003. Now, you might say, okay, but&nbsp;
hold on. Wait a second, David. Whoa, whoa, whoa.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:07.920
The draft opinion from the Supreme Court&nbsp;
makes it just something states can decide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.320
So the polling doesn't really change&nbsp;
because you could be against Roe v Wade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:19.040
but still in favor of the state keeping abortion&nbsp;
legal. Okay. Well, the draft opinion would make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:25.360
it legal for states to make abortion illegal.&nbsp;
And that's ultimately the same thing at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.960
of the day. On the question from the poll, should&nbsp;
Roe v Wade be overturned? And this goes to that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:40.160
Only 30% believe that Roe v Wade should be&nbsp;
overturned. That is up from 23% in 2018. But 63%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:46.320
nearly two thirds of the country believe that&nbsp;
Roe v Wade should not be overturned. That is down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.160
slightly from 2018. Now, there's another question&nbsp;
here. When we think about the political impact of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.200
this forthcoming decision, we've been wondering&nbsp;
what will it do to the midterm elections? Will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:03.280
it inspire Democrats and people on the left to&nbsp;
get out and vote for Democrats, by and large,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.960
for independent voters? We know abortion is not&nbsp;
historically a big voting issue for independents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.080
but what might it do for voter turnout for&nbsp;
Democrats? We haven't had any new polling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.800
that I've been able to find, unlike a generic&nbsp;
congressional ballot for November since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.600
Supreme Court decision leaked. It would be very&nbsp;
interesting to look at that data and hopefully we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.680
will have it soon. The other part of this, by the&nbsp;
way, when we see 60% of Americans believe abortion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:35.120
should be legal in most or all cases, that 60% is&nbsp;
not represented in the Senate. And as I've told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.000
you before, the Senate is a fundamentally&nbsp;
anti-democratic body. You can have in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

